Using hansen_kink_regression.R file, we obtain:

Table 2: Regression Kink Results: IMF common tones
Table C.1: Regression Kink Results: WTO common tones
Table C.2: Regression Kink Results: UN common tones
Table C.3: Regression Kink Results: WB common tones

For each global institution, "gammas" and "dx" parameters are different depending on the sentiment dataset. 
The calculation of these parameters are given in the relevant Excel file:
For IMF: DATA_results_kinkIMF.xlsx
For UN: DATA_results_kinkUN.xlsx
For WB: DATA_results_kinkWB.xlsx
For WTO: DATA_results_kinkWTO.xlsx

The analysis is applied for each country within each Excel file.
Hence, the country code in the "sheet" option changes for the country-based analysis.
For instance, in the function below, we use data for Sweden (SWE) within the DATA_results_kinkUN.xlsx data file:
dataR <- read_excel("DATA_results_kinkUN.xlsx", sheet = "SWE", range = "A25:G118")

******

Using endogenous_kink_regression.R file, we obtain:

Table 3: Endogenous Regression Kink Results: IMF common tones
Table C.4: Endogenous Regression Kink Results: WTO common tones
Table C.5: Endogenous Regression Kink Results: UN common tones
Table C.6: Endogenous Regression Kink Results: WB common tones

For each global institution, "gammas" and "dx" parameters are different depending on the dataset. 
The calculation of these parameters are given in the relevant Excel file:
For IMF: DATA_results_endo_kinkIMF.xlsx
For UN: DATA_results_endo_kinkUN.xlsx
For WB: DATA_results_endo_kinkWB.xlsx
For WTO: DATA_results_endo_kinkWTO.xlsx

The analysis is applied for each country within each Excel file.
Hence, the country code in the "sheet" option changes for the country-based analysis.
For instance, in the function below, we use data for Sweden (SWE) within the DATA_results_endo_kinkUN.xlsx data file:
dataR <- read_excel("DATA_results_endo_kinkUN.xlsx", sheet = "SWE", range = "A25:J118")

******

